enable beat-true-to-sig;

use "@std"
use "@audio"
use "@composition"

Note: ============================================================
Note:  Chapter: Beat Literals & the beat-true-to-sig Pragma (Phase 45)
Note:  Run: dotnet run --project flow-interpreter examples/beat/intro.flow
Note: ============================================================
Note:
Note:  Flow gives Beat values a first-class literal `Nb` — `0.5b`, `2b`,
Note:  `-1b` — matching the rest of the music-type family (`100ms` / `2.5s`
Note:  / `+50c` / `+2st` / `440Hz`). By default `1b = 1 quarter note`,
Note:  matching MIDI/DAW convention.
Note:
Note:  The file-top `enable beat-true-to-sig;` pragma RETUNES the literal to
Note:  the active time signature's beat unit. In `timesig 6/8 { }` with the
Note:  pragma on, `1b = 1 eighth` (multiplier 4/8 = 0.5) — the musician-
Note:  intuition path for a jig "felt in 2 dotted-quarter beats / 6 eighths".
Note:
Note:  The multiplier resolves at CONSTRUCTION time to a quarter-relative
Note:  double; everything downstream (tempo, MIDI ticks, WAV synthesis) stays
Note:  quarter-relative and unchanged.

(print "")
(print "=== Beat Literals & beat-true-to-sig (6/8 jig) ===")
(print "")

tempo 120 {
    timesig 4/4 {

Note: -----------------------------------------------------------
Note: Chapter 1: Default beat unit (4/4 context)
Note: -----------------------------------------------------------
Note: In 4/4 the multiplier is 4/4 = 1.0, so the literal is identity:
Note: 1b stays one quarter. (str Beat) always prints the plain quarter-
Note: relative double — no 'b' suffix (D-14 round-trip lock).
        Beat oneQuarter = 1b
        (print (concat "4/4: 1b = " (str oneQuarter)))
        Beat halfQuarter = 0.5b
        (print (concat "4/4: 0.5b = " (str halfQuarter)))
    }

    timesig 6/8 {

Note: -----------------------------------------------------------
Note: Chapter 2: 6/8 with the pragma — 1b becomes one eighth
Note: -----------------------------------------------------------
Note: denom=8 -> multiplier 4/8 = 0.5. So the SAME `1b` literal now means
Note: one eighth (0.5 quarters); `2b` = one quarter; `0.5b` = one sixteenth.
Note: The composer writes `1b` and gets the eighth-note beat they feel in
Note: 6/8 — no manual `0.5b` conversion needed.
        Beat jigBeat = 1b
        (print (concat "6/8: 1b = " (str jigBeat)))
        Beat twoEighths = 2b
        (print (concat "6/8: 2b = " (str twoEighths)))
        Beat sixteenth = 0.5b
        (print (concat "6/8: 0.5b = " (str sixteenth)))

Note: -----------------------------------------------------------
Note: Chapter 3: render an actual 6/8 jig phrase to MIDI + WAV
Note: -----------------------------------------------------------
Note: The melody is authored with note-stream durations (`e` = eighth,
Note: `q.` = dotted quarter). A 6/8 jig is two groups of three eighths per
Note: bar. This proves the pragma is a pure parse/eval-time desugar: the
Note: rendered audio is ordinary quarter-relative synthesis.
        section jig {
            Sequence melody = | D5e E5e F#5e G5e A5e B5e | A5q. D5q. | D5e E5e F#5e G5e A5e B5e | A5q. F#5q. |
            Sequence bass   = | D3q. A3q. | D3q. A3q. | D3q. A3q. | D3q. D3q. |
        }

        Song jigSong = [jig]
        Buffer jigAudio = (renderSong jigSong "flute")
        (writeWav "/tmp/beat_intro.wav" jigAudio)
        (writeMidi "/tmp/beat_intro.mid" jigSong)
    }
}

(print "")
(print "Rendered: /tmp/beat_intro.wav + /tmp/beat_intro.mid")
(print "examples/beat/intro: PASSED")
